ef_epa_per_play":0.01425},{"week":16,"opponent":"ARI","home":true,"pf":13.0,"pa":27.0,"off_snaps":56,"epa_per_play":-0.247,"pass_rate":0.6429,"pass_rate_neutral":0.5357,"def_epa_per_play":0.1523},{"week":17,"opponent":"SF","home":true,"pf":21.0,"pa":26.0,"off_snaps":70,"epa_per_play":0.1065,"pass_rate":0.5714,"pass_rate_neutral":0.5556,"def_epa_per_play":0.375}],"comps":{"offense":[{"season":2019,"team":"WAS","distance":0.5006,"shared":18,"play_caller":null},{"season":2025,"team":"TB","distance":0.5129,"shared":15,"play_caller":"Josh Grizzard"},{"season":2021,"team":"WAS","distance":0.5541,"shared":18,"play_caller":null},{"season":2024,"team":"CAR","distance":0.5559,"shared":15,"play_caller":"Dave Canales"},{"season":2020,"team":"NYJ","distance":0.5675,"shared":18,"play_caller":null}],"defense":[{"season":2025,"team":"LV","distance":0.8494,"shared":18,"play_caller":"Patrick Graham"},{"season":2018,"team":"MIA","distance":0.9301,"shared":18,"play_caller":null},{"season":2018,"team":"CAR","distance":0.9445,"shared":18,"play_caller":null},{"season":2018,"team":"LA","distance":0.9682,"shared":18,"play_caller":null},{"season":2022,"team":"ARI","distance":1.018,"shared":18,"play_caller":"Vance Joseph"}]},"overview":{"overview":"The Seahawks went 11-5 in 2019 and outscored opponents 405 to 398. The offense ranked 12th in EPA per play and the defense 19th in EPA allowed, so neither side stood out much. What set them apart was the 3rd fewest tight ends on the field and the highest base defense usage in the league.","offense":"The Seahawks offense was a little above average by EPA per play. Their run and pass mix stayed close to what game situations called for. Schematically, they stood out for the 3rd fewest tight ends on the field and the 3rd lowest 13 personnel usage in the league. Russell Wilson ran the passing game, with Tyler Lockett leading the team in targets.","defense":"The Seahawks defense was a little below average by EPA allowed per play. Its strongest area was takeaways per game, where it ranked 4th, and its weakest was success rate allowed, where it ranked 32nd. Jarran Reed was as productive as expected, but the bigger surprise was Rasheem Green, who had 4 sacks despite playing only 51% of the snaps. In terms of scheme, they had the highest base defense usage in the league.","special":"The Seahawks were one of the most conservative teams on fourth down, going for it on only 16% of their short-yardage chances. Their kickers made 82% of field goals, which was about average. On the roster side, they put an unusually large share of their salary cap into linebackers, about 23% of the money on the books.","players":"Russell Wilson played nearly every snap at quarterback and threw for 4,110 yards. Tyler Lockett was the clear top target with 22% of the looks and 1,057 receiving yards. Chris Carson handled most of the work in the backfield with 68% of the carries. On defense, Rasheem Green led the team with 4 sacks and K.J. Wright picked off 3 passes.","coaching":"Brian Schottenheimer called the offense, and the defensive play caller for this season is not in the coaching data. Their offense looked most like the 2019 Commanders and the 2025 Buccaneers under Josh Grizzard. Their defense looked most like the 2025 Raiders under Patrick Graham and the 2018 Dolphins.","trends":"The offense went from 6th in EPA per play in 2018 to 12th in 2019, a modest decline. The defense moved from 11th in EPA allowed per play in 2018 to 19th in 2019, a big step back. The biggest change in approach was Cover 2 rate, which went from 7% to 16%.","notes":"FTN did not start charting plays until 2022, so play action, motion, RPO and screen rates are not available for 2019. Everything else, including personnel, formations and coverage, is complete for this season."}}